---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Privacy Policy — Pegboard Seven"
      description="Everything Pegboard Seven stores or sends, listed event by event."
      current="legal">

  <section>
    <p class="kicker">Pegboard Seven · Privacy Policy</p>
    <h1 style="max-width:22ch">What the game knows about you.</h1>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="prose">
<p>
  Pegboard Seven is a game. It has no accounts, no sign-in, no messaging and no
  profile. We never ask for your name, your email address or your location, and
  there is nowhere in the app to type any of them.
</p>
<p>
  This page describes <strong>version 1.0</strong>. What follows is everything
  the app stores or sends, described plainly.
</p>

<h2>Who is responsible</h2>
<p>
  The data controller is <strong>Nurçin Ehlimanoğlu</strong>, a sole
  proprietorship registered in Türkiye, reachable at
  <a href="mailto:nehlimanoglu@gmail.com">nehlimanoglu@gmail.com</a>. There is
  no data protection officer: the business is one person, and that person reads
  the address above.
</p>

<h2>What stays on your device</h2>
<p>
  Your progress is saved on the phone itself, using Apple's standard storage
  (<code>UserDefaults</code>). It never leaves the device and we cannot read it.
  It holds exactly this:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>which levels you have unlocked, and the stars on each one</li>
  <li>your coin balance</li>
  <li>daily-reward streak and the date it was last claimed</li>
  <li>weekly event progress and which rewards you have taken</li>
  <li>which chapter briefings you have already been shown</li>
  <li>whether sound is on</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Deleting the app deletes all of it, and <em>MORE › Reset Progress</em> clears
  it from inside the game. There is nothing to buy in this version, so there is
  no purchase to restore.
</p>

<h2>What the app sends</h2>

<h3>Analytics — Google Firebase</h3>
<p>
  We record how the game is played so we can tell which levels are unfair and
  where people stop. These are the events, in full:
</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Event</th><th>What it carries</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>level_start</code></td><td>level, whether it is a hard level</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>level_complete</code></td><td>level, stars, moves, screws sent to the tray, coins earned</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>level_fail</code></td><td>level, reason (tray jam or out of moves), moves</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>rescue_offered</code>, <code>rescue_taken</code></td><td>level, reason, coin cost, coin balance</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>booster_used</code></td><td>which booster, coin cost</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>gate_blocked</code>, <code>gate_buy</code></td><td>chapter, stars needed and held, coin cost</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>daily_claim</code>, <code>event_claim</code></td><td>day or milestone, coins received</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>exception</code></td><td>a shortened error message, where in the code it happened, level</td></tr>
</table>
<p>
  None of that identifies you. Firebase itself also collects standard technical
  information we do not choose the contents of — an app-instance identifier,
  your device model and operating system version, and a coarse country or region
  worked out from your IP address. The app is built with Firebase's variant that
  has <strong>no advertising-identifier support</strong>, so the IDFA is never
  read. Firebase's own terms cover the rest; see
  <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase privacy</a>.
</p>

<h3>Crash reports — Firebase Crashlytics</h3>
<p>
  When the game crashes, a report is sent so the fault can be found. It carries
  the technical state of the crash — the stack trace, your device model and
  iOS version, how much memory was free — and the little we add on purpose:
  which level you were on, whether it was a hard level, and your coin balance.
</p>
<p>
  There is no name, no address and no account in a crash report, because the
  game holds none. Crashlytics' own terms cover what Google does with it; see
  <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase privacy</a>.
</p>

<h3>Advertising and purchases</h3>
<p>
  <strong>This version shows no advertisements and sells nothing.</strong> There
  is no advertising SDK and no in-app purchase in the app, the advertising
  identifier is never requested, and iOS will not ask whether the game may track
  you — because it does not.
</p>
<p>
  If a future version introduces either, this page will be updated before that
  version is released, and iOS will ask for your permission where it has to.
</p>

<h2>Why we are allowed to do this</h2>
<p>
  Under the GDPR the basis is <strong>legitimate interest</strong> (Article
  6(1)(f)): knowing which levels are unfair and which crashes are real is what
  makes the game fixable, and it is done with data that identifies nobody. We
  balanced that against your privacy by collecting no contact details, no
  location beyond a country, and nothing you type — because the game gives you
  nothing to type.
</p>
<p>
  You can end that processing at any time by deleting the app. There is no
  account to close, because there was never one to open.
</p>

<h2>How long it is kept</h2>
<p>
  Google Analytics keeps individual events for no longer than
  <strong>14 months</strong> and then deletes them; what survives is the
  aggregate — how many people cleared level nine, not which device did. Crash
  reports are kept by Crashlytics for around 90 days and then removed. Nothing is
  archived elsewhere, and none of it is ever sold, rented, or shared for
  advertising.
</p>

<h2>Where it goes</h2>
<p>
  Both services are run by Google, so the data leaves Türkiye and the EEA and is
  processed in the United States and other countries where Google operates.
  Google covers those transfers with the European Commission's standard
  contractual clauses and its own certification under the EU–US Data Privacy
  Framework; their terms are at
  <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase privacy</a>.
</p>

<h2>Children</h2>
<p>
  Pegboard Seven is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly
  collect anything from them. There is no chat, no user-generated content and no
  advertising. If you believe a child's data has reached us through this app,
  write to <a href="mailto:nehlimanoglu@gmail.com">nehlimanoglu@gmail.com</a> and
  we will remove what we can.
</p>

<h2>Your rights</h2>
<p>
  Rights of access, correction, deletion, objection and portability exist under
  the GDPR, the UK GDPR and Türkiye's KVKK (Law 6698). Because we hold nothing
  that identifies you, there is usually nothing for us to look up, correct or
  delete on request — but write to
  <a href="mailto:nehlimanoglu@gmail.com">nehlimanoglu@gmail.com</a> and we will
  help as far as the data allows.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Stop analytics and crash reports:</strong> delete the app.</li>
  <li><strong>Delete your progress:</strong> <em>MORE › Reset Progress</em>, or
      delete the app.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  You may also complain directly to a supervisory authority — your national
  authority in the EEA, the ICO in the UK, or the Personal Data Protection
  Authority (KVKK) in Türkiye — without contacting us first.
</p>

<h2>If this changes</h2>
<p>
  Advertising and purchases are things this game may gain in a later version.
  Neither can be switched on quietly: each one changes what the app does, and
  <strong>this page is updated before that version ships</strong>, together with
  the App Store privacy label. The date below tells you which version you are
  reading about.
</p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<p>
  <a href="mailto:nehlimanoglu@gmail.com">nehlimanoglu@gmail.com</a> — replies
  within three working days.
</p>

<p class="fine">
  Applies to Pegboard Seven version 1.0. Last updated 11 September 2026.
</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
